**New Evidence**
- Xi’s February 16 call with Macron is the clearest direct 2022 Xi statement favoring negotiation on Ukraine.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Xi Jinping Speaks with French President Emmanuel Macron on the Phone","publisher":"Ministry of Foreign Affairs of the People's Republic of China","publication_date":"2022-02-16","url":"https://www.fmprc.gov.cn/eng/zy/jj/bj2022/zyxw/202202/t20220217_10643016.html","claim":"Xi said relevant parties should stick to political settlement and seek a comprehensive settlement of the Ukraine issue through dialogue and consultation.","locator":"lines 88-89","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official readout of a dated Xi phone call.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period diplomatic conduct.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Xi as head of state and party leader; still a diplomatic readout rather than an operational order.","contrary_evidence":["It is a general diplomatic appeal, not proof of sustained peace leverage.","It did not prevent later coercive moves around Taiwan or continued alignment with Russia."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7"]}

- Xi’s March 18 call with Biden explicitly rejected battlefield confrontation and framed peace as the priority.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Xi Jinping: Countries should not come to the point of meeting on the battlefield. Conflict and confrontation are not in anyone's interest. Peace and security are what the international community should treasure the most.","publisher":"Ministry of Foreign Affairs of the People's Republic of China","publication_date":"2022-03-18","url":"https://www.mfa.gov.cn/eng/gjhdq_665435/3376_665447/3432_664920/3435_664926/202203/t20220318_10653158.html","claim":"Xi said the Ukraine crisis was not something China wanted to see and that countries should not come to the point of meeting on the battlefield.","locator":"opening paragraph / lead summary","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct official Xi readout with explicit peace language and date.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 Ukraine crisis period.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Xi.","contrary_evidence":["The call is declaratory and does not show concrete leverage over Russia.","It coexisted with later Chinese non-condemnation of Russia at the UN."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}

- Xi’s February 25 call with Putin supported negotiations and repeated China’s sovereignty/UN Charter line, but it also accepted Russia’s security framing.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"President Xi Jinping Speaks with Russian President Vladimir Putin on the Phone","publisher":"Ministry of Foreign Affairs of the People's Republic of China","publication_date":"2022-02-25","url":"https://www.fmprc.gov.cn/mfa_eng/gjhdq_665435/3265_665445/3220_664352/3222_664356/202202/t20220225_10645701.html","claim":"Xi said China would support Russia and Ukraine resolving the issue through negotiations and reiterated China's consistent position on respecting sovereignty and the UN Charter.","locator":"lead paragraph / summary of Xi's remarks","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official readout of a dated Xi-Putin phone call.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact target-period response to the invasion's start.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Xi, though the statement is mediated through a bilateral readout.","contrary_evidence":["The call did not publicly condemn Russia's invasion.","It echoed Russian security talking points about NATO and legitimacy of Russian concerns."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.3"]}

- Wang Yi’s five-point Ukraine position is official Chinese policy evidence under Xi-era leadership, emphasizing sovereignty, restraint, and talks.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Wang Yi Expounds China's Five-Point Position on the Current Ukraine Issue","publisher":"Ministry of Foreign Affairs of the People's Republic of China","publication_date":"2022-02-26","url":"https://www.fmprc.gov.cn/eng/wjbzhd/202202/t20220226_10645855.html","claim":"China said sovereignty and territorial integrity should be respected, parties should exercise restraint, and diplomatic efforts toward a peaceful settlement should be supported.","locator":"lines 67-73","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official policy statement, dated immediately after the invasion began.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact early-2022 Ukraine crisis fit.","ruler_attribution":"Indirectly attributable to Xi as top leader of the Chinese state; this is state policy, not a personal command act.","contrary_evidence":["The statement also gave rhetorical space to Russia's 'legitimate security concerns'.","It is not a neutral condemnation of aggression."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}

- China abstained in the Security Council vote on the initial Ukraine resolution, which undercuts the peace language.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia vetoes U.N. Security action on Ukraine as China abstains","publisher":"Reuters, republished by Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-02-25","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/world-news/russia-vetoes-un-security-action-on-ukraine-china-abstains-2772930","claim":"China abstained when the Security Council voted on a resolution that would have deplored Russia's invasion of Ukraine.","locator":"lead paragraph / Reuters opening","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters wire reporting on a major UN vote with direct diplomatic consequences.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact invasion-day international response.","ruler_attribution":"Indirectly attributable to Xi-era state policy, not a personal vote by Xi.","contrary_evidence":["Abstention is less supportive than a veto or yes vote for Russia.","China may have wanted to preserve room for diplomacy."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.7","2B.9"]}

- China again abstained when the General Assembly condemned the invasion, saying the resolution would not be helpful.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"U.N. General Assembly in historic vote denounces Russia over Ukraine invasion","publisher":"Reuters, republished by Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-03-02","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/world-news/un-general-assembly-set-to-censure-russia-over-ukraine-invasion-2775771","claim":"China abstained because it did not believe the resolution would be helpful and said actions should promote de-escalation and an early resumption of dialogue.","locator":"Reuters article lines 432-435 as captured in search/open output","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting directly quotes China's deputy UN ambassador on the abstention rationale.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 war response fit.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect state attribution; this is China's UN line under Xi, not a personal Xi statement.","contrary_evidence":["The stance did not join the international condemnation of aggression.","It can be framed as pro-dialogue, but it still avoided accountability language."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.7","2B.9","2B.10"]}

- The August 3 Taiwan Office statement is overt coercive rhetoric against separatists.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Spokesperson for Taiwan Work Office of CPC Central Committee makes remarks on punishing die-hard 'Taiwan independence' elements","publisher":"English.gov.cn / Xinhua","publication_date":"2022-08-03","url":"https://english.www.gov.cn/news/topnews/202208/03/content_WS62e9d976c6d02e533532ed4a.html","claim":"The statement says die-hard 'Taiwan independence' elements severely jeopardized peace and stability across the Taiwan Straits and must be severely punished in accordance with the law.","locator":"lines 45-52","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official Chinese party-state publication with explicit coercive language.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 Taiwan crisis period.","ruler_attribution":"Indirectly attributable to Xi-era governance and Taiwan policy; the statement is institutional rather than personally signed by Xi.","contrary_evidence":["The document frames the measures as legal and defensive.","It does not itself describe an actual military operation."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.3","2B.6"]}

- The August 10 Taiwan white paper explicitly refused to renounce force.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"China releases white paper on Taiwan question, reunification in new era","publisher":"English.gov.cn / Xinhua","publication_date":"2022-08-10","url":"https://english.www.gov.cn/archive/whitepaper/202208/10/content_WS62f34f46c6d02e533532f0ac.html","claim":"China said it would work for peaceful reunification but would not renounce the use of force and reserved the option of taking all necessary measures.","locator":"lines 63-65","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official white paper with explicit force reservation language.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 policy statement after the Taiwan crisis escalated.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Xi-era state policy and party leadership; not a spontaneous military field decision.","contrary_evidence":["The same text emphasizes peaceful reunification as the stated preference.","It is a declaratory doctrine, not proof of imminent attack intent."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.3","2B.7","2B.10"]}

- Reuters’ Taiwan crisis coverage shows the largest live-fire drills around Taiwan and direct risk escalation after Pelosi's visit.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Furious China fires missiles near Taiwan in drills after Pelosi visit","publisher":"Reuters, republished by Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-08-03","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/stock-market-news/suspected-drones-over-taiwan-cyber-attacks-after-pelosi-visit-2862740","claim":"China deployed large-scale military drills and fired live missiles near Taiwan, sharply raising the risk of regional miscalculation after Pelosi's visit.","locator":"lead paragraph / Reuters opening","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters wire item on a major crisis event with direct operational details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 Taiwan crisis window.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Xi's governing system and Taiwan policy, though not a quoted Xi order.","contrary_evidence":["The drills were conventional rather than nuclear.","They can be read as signaling as well as coercion."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.4","2B.8","2B.10"]}

- China then halted military dialogue and other cooperation with the U.S., cutting crisis channels.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"China halts high-level military dialogue with U.S., suspends other cooperation","publisher":"Reuters, republished by Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-08-05","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/beijing-halts-highlevel-military-dialogue-with-us-suspends-other-cooperation-2864888","claim":"China said it was halting senior-level military dialogue with the United States and suspending other cooperation, including climate talks, in retaliation for Pelosi's Taiwan visit.","locator":"lead paragraph / Reuters opening","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters wire reporting on a specific suspension of channels and mechanisms.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 crisis aftermath.","ruler_attribution":"Indirectly attributable to Xi-era state control over the U.S. relationship and military channels.","contrary_evidence":["Suspending dialogue may have been intended as temporary punishment rather than permanent rupture.","This does not prove all backchannel communication ceased."],"lenses":["2B.7","2B.9","2B.10"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
- I considered roughly 30 documents across official MFA and gov.cn material, Reuters wire coverage, UN voting records, and China’s Taiwan policy texts.
- I retained only non-duplicative items that added a distinct claim about peace, restraint, coercion, dialogue, or crisis management.
- I rejected later retrospective or post-2022 sources unless they were only being used as a clean mirror for a 2022 document, and I avoided broad commentary that did not add a ruler-attributed claim.
- I rejected duplicate republishes of the same Reuters wires once the underlying Reuters reporting was already captured.
- I did not re-fetch the local structured UCDP/SIPRI priors already supplied in the prompt; I used them only as context for the 2022 baseline.

**Remaining Gaps**
- Direct evidence of Xi personally authorizing or restraining specific military moves in the Taiwan crisis, beyond official readouts and policy texts.
- Better evidence on internal deliberation, actual command authority, and whether Xi overruled hawks or empowered them.
- More direct evidence on whether China took concrete steps to mediate the Ukraine war beyond declaratory calls for talks and abstentions at the UN.
- Better documentation of civilian-protection practices during the Taiwan drills, though no actual armed conflict occurred.
- More granular evidence on whether the dialogue suspensions were paired with alternative de-escalation channels.
